  {
   "doc": "patterns.md",
   "chunk_id": "card_not_present_ring",
   "text": "## PATTERN card_not_present_ring\nA card-not-present ring reuses one card (card1) or one purchaser email (P_emaildomain)\nacross many rapid transactions and merchants. Signals: high velocity within one hour,\nC1 (transactions per card window) above 5, shared card1 across 3+ merchants, V1 above 1.5.\nNegative control: a family sharing one card shows low velocity and matching M flags."
  },
  {
   "doc": "patterns.md",
   "chunk_id": "account_takeover",
   "text": "## PATTERN account_takeover\nAccount takeover shows a sudden device change (new DeviceInfo never seen for the card),\naddress or email change (addr2, R_emaildomain), and an amount spike. Signals: D1 near 0\n(first event on new device), dist1 large, M1/M2 flipping T to F. Borderline: travel shows\na roaming device but consistent email and moderate amounts. Negative control: a phone\nupgrade keeps email, address and amount profile stable."
  },
  {
   "doc": "patterns.md",
   "chunk_id": "money_mule_fanout",
   "text": "## PATTERN money_mule_fanout\nMoney-mule fan-out moves funds from one hub card to many recipient emails in rapid\nsuccession, often in round amounts. Signals: 1-to-N topology within minutes, identical\namounts, C2 (recipients per hub) above 5, fresh recipient domains. Borderline: payroll\ndisbursement repeats monthly with stable employee recipients. Negative control:\nmarketplace payouts vary in amount and cadence with established seller identities."
  },
  {
   "doc": "patterns.md",
   "chunk_id": "device_spoofing_cluster",
   "text": "## PATTERN device_spoofing_cluster\nDevice-spoofing clusters run many distinct cards through a single DeviceInfo fingerprint,\ntypical of emulators. Signals: cards-per-device above 5, missing identity fields,\nall-M-F match flags, V1 above 2.5. Borderline: a mall kiosk shares a device but has\nlow velocity and matching geo. Negative control: corporate NAT shares a device across\nemployees with long-established histories and normal amounts."
  },
  {
   "doc": "patterns.md",
   "chunk_id": "synthetic_identity",
   "text": "## PATTERN synthetic_identity\nSynthetic identities combine fabricated identity fragments: thin files (C1 of 1),\nbrand-new device age (low D1), mismatched identity signals and M flags all F.\nSignals: id_ mismatch, rapid early velocity, high amounts for account age. Borderline:\na recent arrival has a thin file but small amounts and consistent geo. Negative control:\na student first card has a thin file with small amounts and a verified university email."
  }
 ],
